<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- The sum identity material below was moved here from 20-circle-trigonometry.ptx. -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-sine-cosine-sum-rules">
  <title>Sum Rules for Sine and Cosine</title>

  <objectives xml:id="obj-sine-cosine-sum-rules">
    <ul>
      <li><p>Interpret adding angles as following one rotation by another, and use the sum identities for sine and cosine to find the coordinates of a rotated point.</p></li>
      <li><p>Apply the sum identities by replacing <m>\sin(\theta)</m>, <m>\cos(\theta)</m>, <m>\sin(\varphi)</m>, and <m>\cos(\varphi)</m> with known coordinate values, to find the sine and cosine of angles such as <m>75^\circ</m>, <m>15^\circ</m>, and <m>\theta+60^\circ</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Use the sum identities in the limit definition of the derivative to write <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\sin(x)</m> and <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\cos(x)</m> in terms of <m>\sin(x)</m>, <m>\cos(x)</m>, and two limits in <m>h</m>, and identify those two limits.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Last time we defined sine and cosine as the coordinates of a point on the unit circle. To
      differentiate these functions we will need to know how <m>\sin(x+h)</m> and
      <m>\cos(x+h)</m> relate to the values at <m>x</m> and at <m>h</m>. We begin by seeing that
      adding angles means following one rotation by another. This gives the sum identities for sine
      and cosine, which we then put to work in the limit definition of the derivative.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-td-rotate-other-points" workspace="0in">
    <title>Rotating Other Starting Points</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Rotating the starting point <m>(1,0)</m> by an angle <m>\theta</m> moves it to the point
        <m>(\cos(\theta),\sin(\theta))</m> on the unit circle. Where do other starting points go
        under the same rotation?
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-td-rotate-two-zero" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Where does the point <m>(2,0)</m> move when it is rotated by the angle <m>\theta</m>?
          Explain your answer using the fact that <m>(2,0)</m> is twice as far from the origin as
          <m>(1,0)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-td-rotate-zero-one" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch the unit circle and the point <m>(0,1)</m>. Rotate this point by an acute angle
          <m>\theta</m> and draw a reference triangle for the new point. Use the triangle to find the
          coordinates of the new point in terms of <m>\cos(\theta)</m> and <m>\sin(\theta)</m>. Pay
          attention to where the angle <m>\theta</m> sits in your triangle and to which side of the
          <m>y</m>-axis the new point lies on.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-td-rotate-zero-three" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Where does the point <m>(0,3)</m> move when it is rotated by the angle <m>\theta</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-td-rotating-by-components">
    <title>Rotating by Components</title>
    <p>
      We can reach a point <m>(a,b)</m> from the origin in two steps: a horizontal displacement
      <m>a</m>, and then a vertical displacement <m>b</m>. As vectors, this says
      <md>(a,b) = (a,0) + (0,b)\text{.}</md>
      Rotation respects this splitting. To rotate the point <m>(a,b)</m>, we can rotate the two
      displacements <m>(a,0)</m> and <m>(0,b)</m> separately, and then add the results.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-td-adding-rotations">
    <title>Adding Angles Means Adding Rotations</title>
    <p>
      Rotate the point <m>(1,0)</m> by an angle <m>\theta</m>, and then continue by rotating through a
      further angle <m>\varphi</m>. The total rotation is by the angle <m>\theta+\varphi</m>, so the
      point we reach is <m>(\cos(\theta+\varphi),\sin(\theta+\varphi))</m>. We can also find this
      point in pieces, following the four pictures below.
    </p>
    <sbsgroup>
      <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="auto" valign="bottom">
        <figure xml:id="fig-td-basic-rotations">
          <caption>Rotating <m>(1,0)</m> and <m>(0,1)</m> by <m>\varphi</m>.</caption>
          <image width="100%">
            <shortdescription>The unit circle with a dashed outline. A blue arc carries the point (1,0) counterclockwise through the angle phi to the point (cos phi, sin phi). A red arc carries the point (0,1) through the angle phi to the point (-sin phi, cos phi). The angle phi is marked between each starting axis and the terminal ray.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=3.2, >=stealth, axis/.style={->, black!70}, font=\footnotesize]
                \colorlet{cosc}{blue!75!black}
                \colorlet{sinc}{red!75!black}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\tD}{30}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\pD}{50}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\pE}{90+\pD}

                \draw[axis] (-1.3,0) -- (1.35,0) node[right] {$x$};
                \draw[axis] (0,-1.3) -- (0,1.35) node[above] {$y$};
                \draw[dashed, black!70] (0,0) circle (1);

                % terminal rays
                \draw[black!50] (0,0) -- (\pD:1);
                \draw[black!50] (0,0) -- (\pE:1);

                % rotation arcs
                \draw[->, very thick, cosc] (1,0) arc (0:\pD:1);
                \draw[->, very thick, sinc] (0,1) arc (90:\pE:1);

                % angle markers
                \draw[cosc] (0.28,0) arc (0:\pD:0.28);
                \node[cosc] at ({\pD/2}:0.42) {$\varphi$};
                \draw[sinc] (90:0.28) arc (90:\pE:0.28);
                \node[sinc] at ({90+\pD/2}:0.42) {$\varphi$};

                % points
                \fill (1,0) circle (0.9pt) node[below right] {$(1,0)$};
                \fill (0,1) circle (0.9pt) node[above right] {$(0,1)$};
                \fill[cosc] (\pD:1) circle (0.9pt)
                    node[above right] {$(\cos\varphi,\ \sin\varphi)$};
                \fill[sinc] (\pE:1) circle (0.9pt)
                    node[above left] {$(-\sin\varphi,\ \cos\varphi)$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-td-rotate-cos-piece">
          <caption>Rotating <m>(\cos(\theta),0)</m> by <m>\varphi</m>.</caption>
          <image width="100%">
            <shortdescription>The point (cos theta, sin theta) on the dotted unit circle, reached by a horizontal blue vector of length cos theta along the x-axis and a vertical vector of length sin theta. A dashed blue circle of radius cos theta carries the end of the blue vector through the angle phi to the point (cos theta cos phi, cos theta sin phi).</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=3.6, >=stealth, axis/.style={->, black!70}, font=\footnotesize]
                \colorlet{cosc}{blue!75!black}
                \colorlet{sinc}{red!75!black}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\tD}{30}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\pD}{50}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\ct}{cos(\tD)}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\st}{sin(\tD)}

                \draw[axis] (-0.35,0) -- (1.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
                \draw[axis] (0,-0.35) -- (0,1.3) node[above] {$y$};
                \draw[dotted, black!50] (0,0) circle (1);

                % circle of radius cos(theta) and the rotation of (cos theta, 0) by phi
                \draw[dashed, cosc] (0,0) circle (\ct);
                \draw[->, thick, cosc] (\ct,0) arc (0:\pD:\ct);

                % vector addition, both ways, to reach (cos theta, sin theta)
                \draw[->, thin, black!60] (\ct,0) -- (\ct,\st);
                \draw[->, thin, black!60] (0,0) -- (0,\st);
                \draw[->, thin, black!60] (0,\st) -- (\ct,\st);
                \draw[->, black] (0,0) -- (\ct,\st);

                % emphasized horizontal component
                \draw[->, line width=2.2pt, cosc] (0,0) -- (\ct,0);

                % labels for the sum
                \fill (\ct,\st) circle (0.5pt)
                    node[above right, xshift=-1pt] {$(\cos\theta,\ \sin\theta)$};
                \node[below, cosc] at ({\ct/2},-0.01) {$\cos\theta$};
                \node[right, black!70] at (\ct,{\st/2}) {$\sin\theta$};
                \draw[black] (0.2,0) arc (0:\tD:0.2);
                \node at ({\tD/2}:0.28) {\small$\theta$};

                % rotation by phi
                \draw[black!50] (0,0) -- (\pD:1.0);
                \draw[cosc] (0.5,0) arc (0:\pD:0.5);
                \node[cosc] at ({\pD/2+8}:0.57) {$\varphi$};
                \fill[cosc] (\ct,0) circle (0.5pt);
                \fill[cosc] ({\ct*cos(\pD)},{\ct*sin(\pD)}) circle (0.5pt)
                    node[left, xshift=-1pt, align=center]
                    {$(\cos\theta\cos\varphi,\ \cos\theta\sin\varphi)$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>
      <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="auto" valign="bottom">
        <figure xml:id="fig-td-rotate-sin-piece">
          <caption>Rotating <m>(0,\sin(\theta))</m> by <m>\varphi</m>.</caption>
          <image width="100%">
            <shortdescription>The point (cos theta, sin theta) on the dotted unit circle, reached by a vertical red vector of length sin theta along the y-axis and a horizontal vector of length cos theta. A dashed red circle of radius sin theta carries the end of the red vector through the angle phi to the point (-sin theta sin phi, sin theta cos phi).</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=3.6, >=stealth, axis/.style={->, black!70}, font=\footnotesize]
                \colorlet{cosc}{blue!75!black}
                \colorlet{sinc}{red!75!black}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\tD}{30}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\pD}{50}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\ct}{cos(\tD)}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\st}{sin(\tD)}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\pE}{90+\pD}

                \draw[axis] (-1.1,0) -- (1.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
                \draw[axis] (0,-0.35) -- (0,1.3) node[above] {$y$};
                \draw[dotted, black!50] (0,0) circle (1);

                % circle of radius sin(theta) and the rotation of (0, sin theta) by phi
                \draw[dashed, sinc] (0,0) circle (\st);
                \draw[->, thick, sinc] (0,\st) arc (90:\pE:\st);

                % vector addition, both ways, to reach (cos theta, sin theta)
                \draw[->, thin, sinc] (\ct,0) -- (\ct,\st);
                \draw[->, thin, black!60] (0,0) -- (\ct,0);
                \draw[->, thin, black!60] (0,\st) -- (\ct,\st);
                \draw[->, black] (0,0) -- (\ct,\st);

                % emphasized vertical component
                \draw[->, line width=2.2pt, sinc] (0,0) -- (0,\st);

                \fill (\ct,\st) circle (0.5pt)
                    node[above right, xshift=-1pt] {$(\cos\theta,\ \sin\theta)$};
                \node[below, black!70] at ({\ct/2},-0.02) {$\cos\theta$};
                \node[right, sinc] at (\ct,{\st/2}) {$\sin\theta$};
                \draw[black] (0.2,0) arc (0:\tD:0.2);
                \node at ({\tD/2}:0.28) {\small$\theta$};

                % rotation by phi (angle measured from the y axis)
                \draw[black!50] (0,0) -- (\pE:0.85);
                \draw[sinc] (90:0.3) arc (90:\pE:0.3);
                \node[sinc] at ({90+\pD/2}:0.40) {\small$\varphi$};
                \fill[sinc] (0,\st) circle (0.5pt);
                \fill[sinc] ({-\st*sin(\pD)},{\st*cos(\pD)}) circle (0.5pt)
                    node[left, xshift=-1pt, align=center]
                    {$(-\sin\theta\sin\varphi,\ \sin\theta\cos\varphi)$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-td-sequential-rotations">
          <caption>Rotating by <m>\theta</m>, then by <m>\varphi</m>, and adding the pieces.</caption>
          <image width="100%">
            <shortdescription>A bold black vector from the origin to the point (cos theta, sin theta) on the dotted unit circle, with dashed blue and red component vectors forming a rectangle. A black arc rotates this point through the angle phi to a second bold black vector. The two rotated components, also dashed in blue and red, form a parallelogram whose far corner is the final point (cos theta cos phi - sin theta sin phi, sin theta cos phi + cos theta sin phi).</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=4, >=stealth, axis/.style={->, black!70}, font=\footnotesize]
                \colorlet{cosc}{blue!75!black}
                \colorlet{sinc}{red!75!black}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\tD}{30}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\pD}{50}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\ct}{cos(\tD)}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\st}{sin(\tD)}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\Ax}{cos(\tD)*cos(\pD)}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\Ay}{cos(\tD)*sin(\pD)}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\Bx}{-sin(\tD)*sin(\pD)}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\By}{sin(\tD)*cos(\pD)}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\Sx}{\Ax+\Bx}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\Sy}{\Ay+\By}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\tp}{\tD+\pD}

                \draw[axis] (-0.5,0) -- (1.25,0) node[right] {$x$};
                \draw[axis] (0,-0.2) -- (0,1.25) node[above] {$y$};
                \draw[dotted, black!50] (0,0) circle (1);

                % first rotation (by theta): components of (cos theta, sin theta), dashed
                \draw[->, dashed, thick, cosc] (0,0) -- (\ct,0);
                \draw[->, dashed, thick, sinc] (\ct,0) -- (\ct,\st);
                \draw[->, dashed, thick, sinc] (0,0) -- (0,\st);
                \draw[->, dashed, thick, cosc] (0,\st) -- (\ct,\st);

                % second rotation (by phi): rotated components and their parallel copies, dashed
                \draw[->, dashed, thick, cosc] (0,0) -- (\Ax,\Ay);
                \draw[->, dashed, thick, sinc] (0,0) -- (\Bx,\By);
                \draw[->, dashed, thick, sinc] (\Ax,\Ay) -- (\Sx,\Sy);
                \draw[->, dashed, thick, cosc] (\Bx,\By) -- (\Sx,\Sy);

                % rotation arc by theta from (1,0) to (cos theta, sin theta)
                \draw[->, thick, black] (1,0) arc (0:\tD:1);

                % rotation arc by phi from (cos theta, sin theta) to the final point
                \draw[->, thick, black] (\tD:1) arc (\tD:\tp:1);

                % bold black vector to (cos theta, sin theta)
                \draw[->, line width=2.2pt, black] (0,0) -- (\ct,\st);

                % bold black final vector
                \draw[->, line width=2.2pt, black] (0,0) -- (\Sx,\Sy);

                % angles: theta from the x axis, phi from the first rotated vector
                \draw[thick, black] (0.25,0) arc (0:\tD:0.25);
                \node at ({\tD/2}:0.33) {\small$\theta$};
                \draw[thick, black] (\tD:0.5) arc (\tD:\tp:0.5);
                \node at ({\tD+\pD/2}:0.38) {\small$\varphi$};

                % only the final point is labeled
                \fill (\Sx,\Sy) circle (0.4pt)
                    node[above right, xshift=-2pt, align=left]
                    {$(\cos\theta\cos\varphi-\sin\theta\sin\varphi,$\\
                      $\ \sin\theta\cos\varphi+\cos\theta\sin\varphi)$};
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>
    </sbsgroup>
    <p>
      The basic rotations by <m>\varphi</m> are in <xref ref="fig-td-basic-rotations"/>. After the
      rotation by <m>\theta</m> we are at
      <m>(\cos(\theta),\sin(\theta)) = (\cos(\theta),0)+(0,\sin(\theta))</m>. Rotating each piece
      by <m>\varphi</m> gives the two points in <xref ref="fig-td-rotate-cos-piece"/> and
      <xref ref="fig-td-rotate-sin-piece"/>:
      <md>
        <mrow> (\cos(\theta),0) \amp \longrightarrow \bigl(\cos(\theta)\cos(\varphi),\ \cos(\theta)\sin(\varphi)\bigr) </mrow>
        <mrow> (0,\sin(\theta)) \amp \longrightarrow \bigl(-\sin(\theta)\sin(\varphi),\ \sin(\theta)\cos(\varphi)\bigr) </mrow>
      </md>
      Adding the two rotated pieces, as in <xref ref="fig-td-sequential-rotations"/>, reaches the point
      <md>
        \bigl(\cos(\theta)\cos(\varphi)-\sin(\theta)\sin(\varphi),\ \sin(\theta)\cos(\varphi)+\cos(\theta)\sin(\varphi)\bigr)\text{.}
      </md>
      This is the same point as <m>(\cos(\theta+\varphi),\sin(\theta+\varphi))</m>, so the coordinates
      must match.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-td-sum-identities">
    <title>Sum Identities for Sine and Cosine</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For all angles <m>\theta</m> and <m>\varphi</m>,
        <md>
          <mrow> \sin(\theta+\varphi) \amp = \sin(\theta)\cos(\varphi)+\cos(\theta)\sin(\varphi) </mrow>
          <mrow> \cos(\theta+\varphi) \amp = \cos(\theta)\cos(\varphi)-\sin(\theta)\sin(\varphi)\text{.} </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <remark xml:id="rem-td-replace-not-substitute">
    <title>Replace the Four Quantities</title>
    <p>
      In <xref ref="thm-td-sum-identities"/>, the four quantities <m>\sin(\theta)</m>,
      <m>\cos(\theta)</m>, <m>\sin(\varphi)</m>, and <m>\cos(\varphi)</m> are numbers: they are the
      coordinates of two points on the unit circle. We use the identities by <em>replacing</em> each
      of these four quantities with its known coordinate value. We never put a coordinate value
      inside a sine or cosine. For example, to find <m>\sin(45^\circ+30^\circ)</m> we do not
      evaluate <m>\sin\bigl(\tfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}+\tfrac{1}{2}\bigr)</m>. We replace
      <m>\sin(45^\circ)</m> by <m>\tfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m>, replace <m>\cos(30^\circ)</m> by
      <m>\tfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</m>, and so on, and then combine the numbers.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-td-adding-rotations" workspace="0in">
    <title>Adding Rotations in Practice</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Use the values of sine and cosine at <m>30^\circ</m>, <m>45^\circ</m>, and <m>60^\circ</m>
        from <xref ref="assemblage-uc-special-triangles" text="title"/>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-td-sum-ninety" workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Take <m>\theta=60^\circ</m> and <m>\varphi=30^\circ</m>. Write down the four numbers that
          replace <m>\sin(\theta)</m>, <m>\cos(\theta)</m>, <m>\sin(\varphi)</m>, and
          <m>\cos(\varphi)</m>, and then use <xref ref="thm-td-sum-identities"/> to compute
          <m>\sin(90^\circ)</m> and <m>\cos(90^\circ)</m>. Do they agree with the point you found for
          <m>90^\circ</m> earlier?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-td-sum-seventy-five" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write <m>75^\circ</m> as the sum of two angles from our special triangles. Use
          <xref ref="thm-td-sum-identities"/> to find <m>\sin(75^\circ)</m> and
          <m>\cos(75^\circ)</m> exactly.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-td-sum-fifteen" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What about <m>15^\circ</m>? It is not the sum of two of our special angles, but it is a sum
          if one of the two angles is negative. Use <xref ref="thm-uc-odd-even"/> to find the
          sine and cosine of the negative angle, and then find <m>\sin(15^\circ)</m> and
          <m>\cos(15^\circ)</m> exactly.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-td-rotate-by-sixty" workspace="0in">
    <title>Rotating a Point Further by 60 Degrees</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        An angle <m>\theta</m> has its terminal ray in Quadrant II, and <m>\sin(\theta)=\dfrac{2}{5}</m>. We
        want the coordinates of the point on the unit circle for the angle <m>\theta+60^\circ</m>.
        We do not know the angle <m>\theta</m> itself, and we do not need it.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-td-find-cos-theta" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the unit circle equation and the quadrant to find <m>\cos(\theta)</m> exactly.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-td-sixty-values" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\sin(60^\circ)</m> and <m>\cos(60^\circ)</m> from the reference triangle.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-td-apply-sum" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use <xref ref="thm-td-sum-identities"/> to find <m>\cos(\theta+60^\circ)</m> and
          <m>\sin(\theta+60^\circ)</m>, and state the coordinates of the point for
          <m>\theta+60^\circ</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-td-derivative-of-sine" workspace="0in">
    <title>The Derivative of Sine</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        By the definition of the derivative, the derivative of <m>f(x)=\sin(x)</m> is
        <md>
          \frac{d}{dx}\sin(x) = \lim_{h\to 0}\frac{\sin(x+h)-\sin(x)}{h}\text{.}
        </md>
        The obstacle is <m>\sin(x+h)</m>, the sine of a sum. <xref ref="thm-td-sum-identities"/>
        gives us a way to handle it.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-td-sine-expand" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Apply <xref ref="thm-td-sum-identities"/> to <m>\sin(x+h)</m>, with <m>\theta=x</m> and
          <m>\varphi=h</m>. Write the difference quotient
          <m>\dfrac{\sin(x+h)-\sin(x)}{h}</m> using your result.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-td-sine-regroup" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The expression <m>\sin(x)</m> appears in two of the terms. Regroup so that the difference
          quotient takes the form
          <md>
            \sin(x)\cdot\bigl[\ \cdots\ \bigr] + \cos(x)\cdot\bigl[\ \cdots\ \bigr]
          </md>
          where each bracket is a single fraction with denominator <m>h</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-td-sine-limit-form" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          As <m>h\to 0</m>, the value of <m>x</m> stays fixed. Which parts of your expression depend
          on <m>h</m>, and which do not? Write <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\sin(x)</m> as <m>\sin(x)</m> times one
          limit plus <m>\cos(x)</m> times another limit.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-td-derivative-of-cosine" workspace="0in">
    <title>The Derivative of Cosine</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The derivative of <m>g(x)=\cos(x)</m> is
        <md>
          \frac{d}{dx}\cos(x) = \lim_{h\to 0}\frac{\cos(x+h)-\cos(x)}{h}\text{.}
        </md>
        The plan is the same as for sine, now using the identity for <m>\cos(x+h)</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-td-cosine-regroup" workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Apply <xref ref="thm-td-sum-identities"/> to <m>\cos(x+h)</m>, and regroup the difference
          quotient <m>\dfrac{\cos(x+h)-\cos(x)}{h}</m> so that <m>\cos(x)</m> multiplies one fraction
          with denominator <m>h</m> and <m>\sin(x)</m> multiplies another.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-td-cosine-limit-form" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\cos(x)</m> as <m>\cos(x)</m> times one limit plus (or minus)
          <m>\sin(x)</m> times another limit.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-td-which-limits" workspace="0in">
    <title>Which Limits Do We Need?</title>
    <task xml:id="task-td-name-limits" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compare your results for sine and for cosine. Two limits in <m>h</m> appear in both. Write
          them down, and name them
          <m>A=\displaystyle\lim_{h\to 0}\dfrac{\cos(h)-1}{h}</m> and
          <m>B=\displaystyle\lim_{h\to 0}\dfrac{\sin(h)}{h}</m> if you have not already. Then write
          both <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\sin(x)</m> and <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\cos(x)</m> in terms of <m>A</m>,
          <m>B</m>, <m>\sin(x)</m>, and <m>\cos(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-td-limits-form" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What happens to the numerator and denominator of each limit as <m>h\to 0</m>? Can we find
          <m>A</m> or <m>B</m> by substituting <m>h=0</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
